<p>A full cache has to choose. Keeping the next answer may mean removing one that another request will soon need. The useful question is therefore not how many answers fit, but which answers save enough source work to deserve their space.</p>
<p>The shop’s catalogue still holds every product. Its cache needs only the copies worth keeping. We will give those copies a small memory budget and follow how changing demand affects the choice. Redis supplies a concrete example later in the article; the same questions apply to other cache implementations.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="working-set">
 <h2 id="working-set">The catalogue, the working set and the cache</h2>
 <p>A shop may list thousands of products while most customers look at the same few. Repeated requests can benefit from a cache much smaller than the catalogue.</p>
 <StoryFigure group="capacity" kind="working" number={1} />
 <p>Loading the whole catalogue would save no more work in Figure 1. Products nobody requests need no place in the cache.</p>
 <p>The <strong>working set</strong> is the set of answers in active use over an interval. Choose the interval when measuring it: a minute of lunchtime traffic may use a different set from a day of catalogue browsing. Within that set, repeated answers usually earn their space more readily than answers requested once.</p>
 <p>The cache’s <strong>resident set</strong> is what it actually holds now. That can include yesterday’s popular products and omit today’s. A promotion might shift demand from mugs to bowls without changing the catalogue’s size. Retention rules try to make the residents follow useful demand.</p>
 <p>Count complete answer identities. The mug in another language or currency is another cached card, even though it is the same product. <a href={hrefFor('cache-keys')}>Cache keys</a> explains that boundary. The figures keep those choices fixed so the product names can stand for complete card identities.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="entry-bytes">
 <h2 id="entry-bytes">What one entry costs</h2>
 <p>The stored value is only part of an entry. The cache also holds its key and enough information to find it, expire it and choose whether to keep it. Its memory allocator may reserve a larger block than the entry needs. An in-process cache also pays for the objects and references that make up its representation.</p>
 <p>A small value can therefore cost more memory than its encoded bytes suggest. Figure 2 separates the parts of one illustrative entry. Actual costs depend on the implementation and representation.</p>
 <StoryFigure group="capacity" kind="bytes" number={2} />
 <p>A limit of three entries would admit either three small cards or three large results. A limit in bytes distinguishes them. A catalogue-list result might occupy the room of several product cards. If it is used once, keeping it may buy nothing while displacing several useful copies.</p>
 <p>Estimate from representative groups of answers. Sample ordinary cards, cards with longer names or extra fields, cached absences and larger derived results. Include the complete keys and the format actually stored. Multiplying every entry by one mug card’s size hides the larger answers and any different allocation costs.</p>
 <p>A narrower representation can leave room for more useful copies. Storing only the fields the card needs avoids carrying a full product description on every hit. Compression can reduce stored and transferred bytes, but adds compression on fills and decompression on hits. A maximum entry size is simpler: return an oversized source result without caching it. Its later requests still pay the source cost.</p>

 <h3 id="headroom">Leave room for the rest of the process</h3>
 <p>Measuring every entry still leaves part of the process unaccounted for. Connections need buffers. Commands and encoding need temporary memory. An allocator can retain pages after entries are removed. Replication and persistence, when enabled, add their own activity. The host or container also needs room beyond the cache process.</p>
 <p>A cache allowance is therefore not a promise about total resident memory. Measure the process under the intended connection count and request sizes, including large fills and any enabled background work. Repeat after churn: fill entries, remove many, then refill with different sizes. The remaining gap to the host limit must cover the peaks this deployment can reach.</p>
 <p>There is no useful universal headroom percentage. A small cache with many clients and a large cache with few clients do not have the same proportions. Choose an entry budget that fits the measured process, then check that it still saves enough source work. For a local cache, include the application’s other allocations in that same exercise.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="expiry-eviction">
 <h2 id="expiry-eviction">Expiry does not reserve a place</h2>
 <p><strong>Expiry</strong> makes a copy unusable when its lifetime ends. <strong>Eviction</strong> removes a resident to make room. A card can be evicted while it would still be acceptable to return, so a 30-second TTL does not promise 30 seconds of hits.</p>
 <StoryFigure group="capacity" kind="expiry" number={3} />
 <p>The application must handle both as misses. Eviction loses a reusable answer, not an accepted product change: the source still owns the product. This is why the same eviction budget should not hold irreplaceable application state.</p>
 <p>Logical expiry and physical reclamation are separate events. An implementation can reject an expired entry at lookup and reclaim unused expired entries during background work. Memcached, for example, documents both lookup-time checks and background reclamation. Reaching a deadline need not return all the associated memory to the operating system at that instant.</p>
 <p>Shortening the TTL may reduce retained data, but it can also remove a card just before its next request. That increases source work even when the cache had room to keep it. Choose expiry for the answer’s permitted lifetime, and enforce a memory budget separately. The shared 30-second insertion TTL is not an unconditional source-age guarantee; <a href={hrefFor('freshness')}>Freshness and invalidation</a> explains that promise.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="admission-eviction">
 <h2 id="admission-eviction">Decide what enters and what leaves</h2>
 <p>An <strong>eviction policy</strong> chooses residents to remove. An <strong>admission policy</strong> decides whether an incoming answer should be stored at all. These are different decisions. The source result can be returned to the caller even when the cache declines to keep it.</p>
 <p>Popularity can change while the catalogue stays the same. A policy that favours yesterday’s useful answers may leave too little room for today’s. Compare the rules against the same changing demand.</p>
 <Replay />
 <p>Refusing the catalogue result preserves the two favourites through the brief browse. Their next requests hit instead of rebuilding displaced copies. It would be a different trade if the catalogue result returned often enough to repay the space it occupies.</p>
 <p>This is admission to the cache, after obtaining an answer. It does not protect the source from the request that produced that answer. Limits on how much missing work may run belong to <a href={hrefFor('cache-misses')}>Handling cache misses</a>.</p>

 <h3 id="recent-frequent">Recent use and frequent use</h3>
 <p><strong>Least recently used</strong>, or LRU, removes the entry whose last use is furthest in the past. The model counts filling an entry as a use. Its bet is that a recently requested answer is likely to be requested again. A scan can defeat that bet: each new answer looks recent and can replace a repeatedly useful one.</p>
 <p><strong>Least frequently used</strong>, or LFU, favours entries with more uses. In this trace, the mug and jug have built up a history of repeated use before demand changes. LFU protects them when new products arrive.</p>
 <p>The spoon and bowl then compete for the remaining space. Each leaves before its next request, so neither gains the resident use count needed to displace the old favourites. The policy keeps rewarding demand that has ended.</p>
 <p>The model deliberately never forgets. A practical frequency policy needs to reduce the influence of old requests, through decay or a recent history. Redis’s LFU uses decaying estimated counts. Its LRU and LFU sample candidates rather than choosing the model’s exact victim. Figure 4 therefore explains the predictions behind those policies; it cannot forecast Redis’s next eviction.</p>
 <p>The third rule, Second request, keeps a new answer only if its key appeared in the preceding eight requests. One-off browsing never enters the cache, so it cannot displace the favourites. The price is slower adaptation: a newly popular answer must miss twice before its third request can hit.</p>
 <p>A reuse filter must remember something about answers it did not store. The model keeps eight request identities outside its entry budget. That history also needs bounded memory in an implementation. Caffeine’s <a href="https://github.com/ben-manes/caffeine/wiki/Design">Window TinyLFU design</a> combines a window for recent arrivals with estimated frequency when competing for longer retention. It makes a more nuanced decision than this second-request rule, with bookkeeping of its own.</p>
 <p>None of these rules knows the next request. Recent use adapts quickly but can follow a scan. Frequency can resist that scan but remember old popularity too long. Requiring reuse can reject one-off answers while delaying new useful ones. Judge the combination on the changes your workload actually contains.</p>

 <h3 id="saved-work">Count the work saved by the space</h3>
 <p>Hit count treats every answer as equally valuable. A card that needs a costly join may save much more work per hit than a cheap lookup. In this replay the bowl has a higher source cost; keeping its small card is particularly valuable.</p>
 <p>The default run saves 50 of the 94 work units needed without caching. Its ten hits make unequal contributions:</p>
 <div class="cache-table-scroll"><table>
  <caption>Saved work in Figure 4’s default run</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Answers</th><th scope="col">Space when resident</th><th scope="col">Hits</th><th scope="col">Work saved</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><th scope="row">Mug and jug together</th><td>2 KiB</td><td>4</td><td>4 × 2 = 8</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Spoon</th><td>1 KiB</td><td>3</td><td>3 × 2 = 6</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Bowl</th><td>1 KiB</td><td>3</td><td>3 × 12 = 36</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Catalogue and vase</th><td>3 KiB and 1 KiB</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>The mug and jug produce more hits than the bowl, yet save less work while occupying twice as much space together. A policy that improves the overall hit ratio could still lose the expensive hits. Keep source cost and entry size beside request frequency when evaluating it. Also count the cache work those hits require, as <a href={hrefFor('cache-benefits')}>Deciding what and where to cache</a> does.</p>
 <p>A byte or weight limit does not itself optimise work saved per byte. Caffeine, for example, can constrain entry count or a supplied weight, but its documentation says relative weights do not select eviction victims. A weight bounds occupancy; deciding which answer earns that occupancy is another part of the design.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="redis-budget">
 <h2 id="redis-budget">Put the budget into Redis</h2>
 <p>For Redis, set <code>maxmemory</code> and choose <code>maxmemory-policy</code> explicitly. The following configuration illustrates a disposable cache with a 64 MiB allowance. That number is a starting point for an experiment, not a size recommendation.</p>
 <pre><code>{`# Configuration for an owned disposable Redis instance
maxmemory 64mb
maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru`}</code></pre>
 <p>The <code>allkeys-</code> policies can remove any key. The <code>volatile-</code> policies consider only keys with an expiry; without eligible keys, they cannot make room. <code>noeviction</code> retains keys and rejects writes that need more memory, while reads can continue. Keep state that must survive outside this disposable budget. Redis’s <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/reference/eviction/">eviction documentation</a> defines the full policy set.</p>
 <p>The collection’s native lab forced the refusal path on Redis 8.10.3 by selecting <code>noeviction</code> and lowering <code>maxmemory</code> to one byte. A new <code>SET … EX 30</code> then returned an OOM error. The application still held its successful source result: the mug at revision 2, GBP 22.00. It could return that result despite the failed fill.</p>
 <p>This deliberately impossible limit tests error handling. It does not measure how many cards a useful cache can hold, or prove any eviction policy’s quality. The source in this experiment was a Python dictionary; the Redis rejection was observed on an owned local instance. <a href={hrefFor('redis')}>Caching with Redis</a> provides the runnable lab and the application adapter that keeps the source result when an optional fill fails.</p>

 <h3 id="measure-memory">Measure entries and the process separately</h3>
 <p>After loading representative answers into a disposable instance, inspect both a key and the process:</p>
 <pre><code>{`MEMORY USAGE product:v1:north:P7:en-GB:GBP
INFO memory
INFO stats
INFO commandstats`}</code></pre>
 <p><code>MEMORY USAGE</code> reports allocations for the key, value and administrative overhead. For nested data types it samples elements by default; <code>SAMPLES 0</code> examines all of them. Repeat across the size groups, and record the Redis version, encoding and allocator with the results. Do not substitute Figure 2’s 1 KiB assumption for those measurements.</p>
 <div class="cache-table-scroll"><table>
  <caption>Different questions in INFO memory</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><th scope="row"><code>used_memory</code></th><td>Bytes Redis has allocated through its allocator.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><code>used_memory_dataset</code></th><td>That allocation total with Redis’s reported management overhead subtracted.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><code>used_memory_rss</code></th><td>The process’s resident memory as seen by the operating system.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><code>mem_not_counted_for_evict</code></th><td>Memory excluded from the eviction calculation.</td></tr></tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Summing per-key estimates does not account for the whole process. Nor does a high ratio of RSS to allocated bytes prove allocator fragmentation: process overhead contributes too. Look at the byte amounts, the allocator fields and how they change through the load.</p>
 <p>Some replication and AOF buffers are excluded from Redis’s eviction calculation. Large operations can also exceed the configured allowance temporarily. Leave measured headroom beyond <code>maxmemory</code>, and check the whole deployment during its enabled persistence or replication work.</p>
 <p>The accounting changes with the implementation. Memcached places items into allocation size classes, so a slightly larger value can need a larger chunk. A local Caffeine weight limit reflects the application’s weight function, not an automatic measurement of the Java heap. Use the implementation’s measurements to connect the abstract entry budget to actual memory.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="evaluate">
 <h2 id="evaluate">Check the choice against changing requests</h2>
 <p>Start with a trace of complete keys, arrival times, answer sizes and source costs. Record updates, invalidations and expiry as well: a repeated key cannot hit if its previous copy is no longer usable. Keep the cold start in the trace and include periods of steady demand, scanning and changed popularity.</p>
 <p>Hold those inputs fixed while changing one choice. Figure 4’s model makes a small example. Keep LRU and the 1 KiB entry limit, then compare its budgets:</p>
 <div class="cache-table-scroll"><table>
  <caption>The same 18 requests, with the catalogue result excluded by size</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Budget</th><th scope="col">Hits</th><th scope="col">Source work remaining</th><th scope="col">Work saved</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{capacityRows.map(row=><tr><th scope="row">{row.capacity} KiB</th><td>{row.hits}</td><td>{row.work}</td><td>{row.saved}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Moving from 2 to 3 KiB lets the mug and jug survive the vase request, saving two more reads and four work units. Going to 5 or 8 KiB saves nothing further on this trace. Every remaining miss is the first request for its answer, including the catalogue result, which the size rule excludes. More room cannot remove those first source reads.</p>
 <p>Now hold 3 KiB fixed and compare policies. With the same entry-size limit, the model’s LFU saves only 12 work units because it keeps the old popular cards. Second request saves 36, and LRU saves 54. Those results select a candidate for this trace; they do not rank the algorithms for every workload.</p>
 <p>Replay production-shaped requests against the chosen implementation on a disposable deployment. Preserve concurrency and arrival timing, and measure application latency, useful hits and source work as well as memory. A sequential model cannot reproduce delayed fills, concurrent requests or Redis’s sampled victim choices. Compare repeated runs rather than treating one exact eviction order as a requirement.</p>
 <p>Read counter differences over the same interval. In Redis, <code>evicted_keys</code> and <code>expired_keys</code> distinguish two reasons entries leave. Command rejection counts and application fill failures expose another reason useful copies never arrive. The application must still decide whether a found value is usable; a server hit is not necessarily saved source work.</p>
 <p>Write down what would overturn the choice. If extra memory removes few source reads, reconsider buying it. If a size limit repeatedly rejects expensive reusable answers, change the representation, threshold or allocation between answer types. If old popularity dominates after a shift, test a policy that forgets faster. If process peaks approach the host limit, reduce the budget or change the deployment before relying on a healthy hit ratio.</p>
 <p>For this invented trace, 3 KiB with LRU and a 1 KiB entry limit is enough to capture all the available repeated reads. A real choice needs the same argument with measured bytes and a representative history. <a href={hrefFor('operating-caches')}>Operating a cache</a> carries those measurements into diagnosis, rollout and recovery as requests continue to change.</p>
</section>

